          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following best characterises the chronic phase of eczema?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following best characterises the chronic phase of eczema?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Redness, swelling, and vesicles that weep and crust over</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These features — redness, vesicles, oozing, and crusting — define the acute phase of eczema, not the chronic phase.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Drying of the lesions with the onset of scaling</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Drying and scaling characterise the subacute phase, which is transitional between acute and chronic eczema.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thickened, leathery skin with exaggerated surface markings (lichenification)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — chronic eczema produces lichenification, where persistent scratching thickens the skin and accentuates its surface markings. Itch drives this cycle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spontaneous clearing of lesions with residual hypopigmentation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic eczema does not resolve spontaneously. The itch–scratch cycle sustains the disease, and active management is needed to break it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. Chronic eczema is defined by lichenification — thickened skin with exaggerated surface markings — resulting from persistent scratching. Acute eczema shows vesicles and oozing; subacute shows drying and scaling."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Lichenification — thickened, leathery skin with exaggerated markings — is the hallmark of chronic eczema, produced by the persistent itch–scratch cycle."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
