        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 282.3,
            "to": 292.3
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child with flexural atopic eczema has lesions on the face. Which topical agent</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child with flexural atopic eczema has lesions on the face. Which topical agent is most appropriate for the facial lesions?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Potent fluorinated corticosteroid</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Potent steroids on the face cause skin atrophy, telangiectasia, and steroid rosacea. They are reserved for thickened skin on the palms and soles.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Moderate-potency corticosteroid</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Moderate-potency steroids are suitable for the trunk and limbs but are still too strong for the thin skin of the face.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mild hydrocortisone or a topical calcineurin inhibitor</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — the face and flexures need mild hydrocortisone or steroid-sparing calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus or pimecrolimus), which control inflammation without thinning the skin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Over-the-counter combination cream containing a potent steroid and antifungal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Combination creams with potent steroids are a common cause of harm in practice. On the face, they cause atrophy and can mask fungal infections (tinea incognito).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. On the face, only mild hydrocortisone or calcineurin inhibitors should be used. Potent steroids cause atrophy, rosacea, and other adverse effects on thin facial skin."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Mild hydrocortisone or calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, pimecrolimus) are the appropriate choice for facial eczema, avoiding the skin-thinning effects of stronger steroids."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
