          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 28-year-old man develops fever, facial swelling, lymphadenopathy, and a widesp</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old man develops fever, facial swelling, lymphadenopathy, and a widespread rash five weeks after starting carbamazepine. Blood tests show eosinophilia and raised liver enzymes. Which adverse drug reaction does this presentation most likely represent?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stevens-Johnson syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SJS presents with painful skin detachment (&lt;10% BSA), a positive Nikolsky sign, and mucosal erosions — not facial swelling and eosinophilia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fixed drug eruption</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fixed drug eruption presents as a round dusky plaque recurring at the same site on re-exposure, without systemic features like lymphadenopathy or hepatitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — the long latency (2–6 weeks), facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, and hepatitis are hallmarks of DRESS. Carbamazepine is a well-known culprit among aromatic anticonvulsants.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maculopapular drug rash</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A maculopapular rash is the commonest drug eruption but presents as a widespread measles-like rash without facial oedema, eosinophilia, or organ involvement.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. This is DRESS — slow onset (2–6 weeks), facial swelling, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, and hepatitis are its cardinal features. Remember: DRESS means slow onset plus systemic injury."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. DRESS is defined by its delayed onset (2–6 weeks), facial oedema, eosinophilia, and organ involvement — most commonly hepatitis. The long latency makes the drug easy to overlook."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
