          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In managing a patient with toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN), which of the follow</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In managing a patient with toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN), which of the following should be avoided?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intravenous fluid resuscitation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IV fluids are essential in TEN to replace the massive losses from denuded skin, managed as for a major burn.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Systemic corticosteroids</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — systemic corticosteroids are avoided in TEN because benefit is unproven and harm is possible. T-cell–mediated damage persists for days after drug withdrawal, making supportive burns-protocol care the mainstay.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Non-adherent wound dressings</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Non-adherent dressings protect the exposed dermis and are a standard part of TEN burns-protocol management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urgent ophthalmology referral</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ophthalmology referral is essential because mucosal erosions of the eyes can lead to scarring, symblepharon, and permanent visual impairment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. Systemic corticosteroids should be avoided in TEN because benefit is unproven and harm is possible. The correct approach is supportive burns-protocol care: IV fluids, non-adherent dressings, warmth, analgesia, and ophthalmology referral."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Systemic corticosteroids are not recommended in TEN — their benefit is unproven and they may worsen outcomes. Management follows a burns protocol: fluids, non-adherent dressings, warmth, analgesia, and urgent specialist referral."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
